 "assumptions":
 [{"text":
   "The eight possible patterns of mud are world objects; muddy and clean say each child's forehead in each world; view-mate(c, w, w2) holds when w2 differs from w only on c's own forehead (what c cannot see). The actual world is w-mmc.",
   "stmts": [1, 2],
   "kind": "intent",
   "id": "A-MC1"},
  {"text":
   "Knowledge is explicit (JL, 16 Nov 2026): knows-at(c, w, r) holds when, in world w at round r, no world c cannot tell from w is still possible. It and the other derived relations use negation only in their definitions; operators test them positively.",
   "stmts": [2, 4, 5],
   "kind": "intent",
   "id": "A-MC2"},
  {"text":
   "The father's statement and each round are operators that rule out worlds (out-at(w, round)), on every world at once. A world is live at round r if it was not ruled out in an earlier round, so each round is judged on the worlds left before it. A stage number keeps the order; round objects carry their number.",
   "stmts": [3, 4, 5],
   "kind": "intent",
   "id": "A-MC3"},
  {"text":
   "A round records which children answer yes in the actual world (said-yes(c, r)), and rules out every world where some child would answer differently than in the actual world (all answers are heard).",
   "stmts": [4],
   "kind": "intent",
   "id": "A-MC4"},
  {"text":
   "The goal is that every child has answered yes in some round; the rounds of first yes are read from said-yes. Four rounds are provided.",
   "stmts": [6],
   "kind": "intent",
   "id": "A-MC5"}]}
